Storm! Engine v2 @VERSION@ — Windows (x64) SDK
==============================================

Built with MinGW-w64 (x86_64-w64-mingw32, posix threads). This is a
cross-compile from Linux; nothing here was produced by MSVC.

Contents
--------

  bin/                 libstormenginev2.dll and every DLL it imports —
                       SDL2 and satellites, plus the MinGW C++ runtime.
  lib/                 import libraries: libstormenginev2.dll.a, and SDL2's
                       (libSDL2.dll.a, libSDL2main.a, libSDL2_image.dll.a,
                       libSDL2_ttf.dll.a, libSDL2_mixer.dll.a) plus
                       libtinyxml2.a. SDL2's are here because your game calls
                       SDL directly and a program cannot borrow its library's
                       transitive imports.
  include/stormengine2 the engine headers, plus tinyxml2.h.
  include/SDL2         SDL2 headers.
  include/glm          glm headers.
  template/            a starter game.

SDL2, glm and tinyxml2 headers are included because the engine's own headers
need them and Windows has no package manager to fetch them from: 12 engine
headers include <SDL2/SDL.h> and 6 include <glm/...>.

Building a game against it
--------------------------

With MinGW-w64:

  g++ -std=c++17 mygame.cpp \
      -Iinclude \
      -Llib -lstormenginev2 \
      -o mygame.exe

Then put the contents of bin\ beside mygame.exe, or on PATH. Windows resolves
DLLs at load time from the executable's own directory first; a missing one
gives no useful message, so copy the whole directory rather than picking.

MSVC is NOT supported. The import library and the C++ ABI are GCC's, so a
program compiled with cl.exe cannot link this build. Use MinGW-w64, or build
the engine from source with your own toolchain.

tinyxml2
--------

tinyxml2 is compiled INTO libstormenginev2.dll (statically) and its header
ships here. Do not also link a separate tinyxml2: XmlLoader embeds an
XMLDocument by value, so your translation unit emits ~XMLDocument and has to
agree with the engine on the layout. Two copies of different vintages is an
ODR violation that links cleanly and misbehaves at runtime.

